---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const breadcrumbLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  'itemListElement': [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', 'position': 1, 'name': 'Home', 'item': 'https://portfolioatlas.org' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', 'position': 2, 'name': 'About', 'item': 'https://portfolioatlas.org/about' },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title="About"
  description="Learn about PortfolioAtlas — how we calculate FIRE lifestyle costs and why we built this resource."
  jsonLd={breadcrumbLd}
>
  <div class="container-narrow py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-brand-950 mb-6">
        About PortfolioAtlas
      </h1>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-6">
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          PortfolioAtlas answers one simple question: <strong>"If I retire with $X, what kind of
          life can I actually live?"</strong> We break it down city by city, dollar by dollar.
        </p>

        <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-brand-950 mt-12 mb-4">How We Calculate</h2>
        <p>
          By default we use the widely-accepted <strong>4% safe withdrawal rate</strong>
          (the "Trinity Study" rule) to determine the <strong>FIRE Number</strong> — the portfolio
          value needed to sustain a given lifestyle in a given city indefinitely. Changing this rate
          on the calculator resizes that reference target, but whether a city counts as "you can
          retire here" is decided by the historical backtest below — not the rule.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-brand-50 rounded-xl p-6 my-6 border border-brand-100">
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 text-center mb-4">
            <strong>FIRE Number</strong> = Annual Living Cost &divide; 0.04 = Monthly Cost &times; 300
          </p>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 text-center">
            <div>
              <div class="text-sm text-gray-500">Lean FIRE</div>
              <div class="text-xs text-gray-400">Modest living, local transport</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="text-sm text-gray-500">FIRE</div>
              <div class="text-xs text-gray-400">Nice apartment, dining out regularly</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="text-sm text-gray-500">Fat FIRE</div>
              <div class="text-xs text-gray-400">Premium living, frequent travel</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          For each lifestyle tier, we research and estimate realistic monthly costs for housing, groceries,
          dining out, healthcare, transportation, entertainment, utilities, and — at higher
          tiers — domestic help and luxury extras. The FIRE Number tells you the portfolio
          value needed to cover those costs at your chosen withdrawal rate (4% by default).
        </p>

        <h2 id="fire-score" class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-brand-950 mt-12 mb-4 scroll-mt-24">The FIRE Score</h2>
        <p>
          Each city receives a <strong>FIRE Score from 1 to 10</strong> based on a weighted
          combination of four quality-of-life factors: safety (30%), healthcare quality (30%),
          infrastructure (20%), and expat friendliness (20%). Cost of living is intentionally
          excluded — it's already addressed through the affordability filters and lifestyle tiers.
        </p>
        <p>
          A higher score means a better quality of life for retirees. Cities like Singapore,
          Tokyo, and Amsterdam score highest thanks to excellent safety, world-class healthcare,
          and strong infrastructure. The FIRE Score helps you answer: "If I can afford this city,
          how good is it to actually live there?"
        </p>

        <h2 id="confidence" class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-brand-950 mt-12 mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Retirement confidence: a historical backtest</h2>
        <p>
          The 4% safe withdrawal rate comes from the <strong>Trinity Study</strong> and is generally
          validated for retirements of about 30 years. It's the default we use to size each city's
          FIRE Number, and you can adjust it on the calculator.
        </p>
        <p>
          On each city page, the <strong>retirement confidence</strong> figure is a real historical
          backtest — not a rule of thumb. We run your plan through <strong>every</strong>
          retirement-length window in US market history using Robert Shiller's dataset
          (<strong>1871–2022</strong>): a 75% stock / 25% bond portfolio, rebalanced annually, with
          withdrawals raised each year for that period's actual inflation. The
          <strong>success rate</strong> is the share of those historical start years in which the
          portfolio lasted the full length without running out.
        </p>
        <p>
          A longer horizon lowers the success rate — early retirees planning 40–50+ years will see
          fewer historical periods survive than the 30-year Trinity baseline. That's exactly why a
          plan can clear the 4% rule yet still show a sub-90% confidence. A more conservative
          withdrawal rate raises your target but improves the odds.
        </p>

        <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-brand-950 mt-12 mb-4">Important Disclaimers</h2>
        <p>
          The cost data on PortfolioAtlas is researched and estimated for informational purposes
          only. Actual costs vary based on personal lifestyle choices, exchange rate fluctuations,
          inflation, and individual circumstances.
        </p>
        <p>
          This site does not provide financial, tax, or legal advice. Always consult with qualified
          professionals — a financial advisor, tax specialist, and immigration attorney — before
          making any relocation or retirement decisions.
        </p>
        <p>
          Cost data reflects approximate values as of early 2026 and will be periodically reviewed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
